# Gen Alpha Bridging Model

A mathematical futurology report on building diverse friend groups and integrating newcomers

Status of the numbers: every parameter below is illustrative. The structure of the model is the useful part. Real calibration should use data like Add Health, Chetty et al.'s economic connectedness studies, and local school and neighborhood records.

---

## 1. The problem in one equation

Gen Alpha (born roughly 2010 to 2024) is about 55 million people in the US. Their friend groups will be set mostly between ages 6 and 20, so the intervention window is already open.

Friendship has two separable causes, which Chetty and colleagues call exposure and friending bias:

- Exposure (s): the share of people in your daily contact set who are in your own group.
- Homophily (H): the extra tendency to befriend your own group once you are in the same room. H = 0 means friends are picked at random from the room. H = 1 means only own-group friends.

The share of your friends who are in your own group is:

w = s + H(1 - s)

So the out-group friend share is:

x = (1 - s)(1 - H)

Two levers follow directly. Lower s by changing who is in the room. Lower H by changing how people interact once they are there.

---

## 2. Why attitudes alone fail: the Schelling result

In Schelling-type models, agents who only mildly prefer own-type neighbors (roughly 30 to 40 percent) still produce heavily segregated outcomes. Individually reasonable, even tolerant, preferences aggregate into sorted schools, congregations, and friend groups.

Implication: you cannot count on kids or parents simply becoming more open-minded. You have to design the contact structure so that mixing is the default outcome.

---

## 3. Contact dose: how many hours make a friend

Model the chance that two kids become close friends after T hours together as:

p(T) = 1 - exp(-lambda * T)

Assume a same-group rate of lambda = 0.004 per hour, and an out-group rate of lambda * (1 - H) * m, where m is a multiplier for cooperative, equal-status, shared-goal contact (Allport's conditions). Take H = 0.6 and a 150-hour season, the size of a typical team sport or school play.

Same group: lambda 0.0040 per hour, 45% friend probability after 150 hours
Out-group, ordinary contact (m = 1): 0.0016 per hour, 21%
Out-group, cooperative contact (m = 2): 0.0032 per hour, 38%

Takeaway: cooperative contact closes most of the gap between same-group and out-group friendship, and one season does most of the work. Kids sitting near each other in class are not in this regime.

---

## 4. The time-budget model for a newcomer kid

Consider a hypothetical Somali-American teenager whose out-of-school life is concentrated in the community. Her friend-formation weight in each setting is proportional to the time she spends there. For each setting i:

x = sum of tau_i * (1 - s_i) * (1 - H_i)

where tau is the share of time in that setting.

Baseline:

- Community and home networks: time 50%, own-group share 0.90, homophily 0.50, yield 0.05, contribution 0.025
- School (tracked): time 40%, s 0.35, H 0.60, yield 0.26, contribution 0.104
- Mixed activities: time 10%, s 0.20, H 0.30, yield 0.56, contribution 0.056
- Total out-group friend share: 18.5%

Intervention: free mixed activities (sports, robotics, theater, service) take 30% of time, community keeps 30%, and school detracking lowers school H to 0.45.

- Community and home networks: time 30%, yield 0.05, contribution 0.015
- School (less tracked): time 40%, yield 0.36, contribution 0.143
- Mixed activities: time 30%, yield 0.56, contribution 0.168
- Total out-group friend share: 32.6%

That is about 1.76 times the baseline, and she still has a majority in-group friend circle (about 67%), which keeps the community's social support intact. The goal is bridging, not dissolving.

Sensitivity (partial derivatives):

- Shifting 10 points of time from community to mixed activities adds about +5.1 points of out-group friend share.
- Lowering school homophily by 0.1 adds about +2.6 points.

Time allocation is a stronger lever than attitude change, which is good news because time is cheaper to change than attitudes.

